Draw the **Lattice item icons**, a 17-frame **sprite sheet** of the inventory items
in a factory simulation, using only the drawing tool.

Each frame is **one distinct item** — eight base materials (an ore, a plate, a gear,
a coil of cable, a circuit board, and a lump of coal) and nine placeable machines (a
belt, an assembler, and an inserter, each in three tiers) — drawn as a tiny,
instantly-recognizable icon. The seventeen together form one cohesive icon family,
and their order is fixed by the brief.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. You
build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a
time**, each targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you
issue are recorded to a per-frame action log, and that log is your submission.

Each frame is its **own separate 32×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 0 --x 3 --y 6 --width 10 --height 4 --color "#b9c0cb"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and
decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the icon set up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png`
matches the brief: seventeen small, mutually-distinguishable item icons — seven of
the eight base materials in frames 0-6 (the eighth, coal, at frame 16), and the nine
tiered machines (belt, assembler, and inserter, each in three tiers) in frames 7-15 —
each centred in its own 32×32 frame with a consistent dark outline and top-left
lighting. When you are satisfied, stop:
the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished submission.
